5.1 Introduction

The Project Development Phase involves implementing the ComicCraft application using Python, FastAPI, Gemini AI, Stable Diffusion, HTML, CSS, and Jinja2.

The application is divided into independent modules to simplify development, debugging, maintenance, and integration.

5.2 Development Environment Setup

First, install Python and Visual Studio Code. Open the project folder in VS Code and create a virtual environment.

mkdir ComicCraft
cd ComicCraft

python -m venv env

For Windows:

env\Scripts\activate

Install the required packages:

pip install fastapi uvicorn jinja2 python-multipart
pip install google-generativeai diffusers transformers
pip install torch fpdf pillow accelerate

The precise package versions and compatible API libraries should be confirmed before implementation.

5.3 Backend Development

The backend is developed using FastAPI. It receives user input, calls AI generation functions, organizes the generated comic, and handles PDF export.

Main Application – main.py
from fastapi import FastAPI
from app.routes import router

app = FastAPI(
    title="ComicCraft",
    description="AI Comic Story Creator",
    version="1.0"
)

app.include_router(router)

This creates the FastAPI application and connects the project routes.

Story Outline Generation – gemini_flash.py

The generate_outline() function uses Gemini Flash to produce a structured comic outline based on the user's prompt.

Its expected output contains five panels. Each panel includes:

Panel number

Panel title

Scene description

Image generation prompt

The outline provides the structure for the detailed story and illustrations.

Story Generation – gemini_pro.py

The generate_story() function expands the outline into narration and character dialogues.

It receives the panel outline and uses Gemini Pro to generate the story content in a structured format suitable for the comic preview.

Image Generation – image_generator.py

The generate_image() function uses Stable Diffusion to create comic illustrations based on the image prompts.

Generated images are saved in the static/panels directory, and their paths are returned for use in the comic layout.

Comic Layout – layout_builder.py

The build_comic_layout() function matches each generated image with its corresponding panel text.

The resulting layout contains the panel number, image path, and story content in the correct sequence.

PDF Export – exporters.py

The save_pdf() function uses FPDF to generate the final comic PDF.

It places panel images and narrative text into a structured page layout and saves the generated file in the static/exports directory.

5.4 Frontend Development

The frontend is built with HTML, CSS, and Jinja2 templates.

1. Homepage – index.html

The homepage includes input fields for the story prompt, character name, setting, tone, and art style. Users submit the form to start comic generation.

2. Comic Preview – comic_preview.html

This page displays the generated comic panel-by-panel, including illustrations, titles, scene descriptions, captions, and narration.

3. Export Success – export_success.html

This page confirms the PDF export process and provides an option to return to the homepage to create another comic.

